00:01:33.845 --> 00:01:36.095
Olav Schewe is joining us today

00:01:36.095 --> 00:01:38.445
and we're looking forward
to talking about learning.

00:01:38.445 --> 00:01:40.035
Welcome Olav.

00:01:40.035 --> 00:01:41.505
- Thank you.

00:01:41.505 --> 00:01:43.915
- It is great to have you here.

00:01:43.915 --> 00:01:47.635
So first as we get started,
I'd like to just find out

00:01:47.635 --> 00:01:52.435
from people how they got to
be interested in learning.

00:01:52.435 --> 00:01:54.695
Most of us were students at some points

00:01:54.695 --> 00:01:58.075
and struggled at different
points with learning

00:01:58.075 --> 00:02:00.065
and how that came in, but
not everyone takes that

00:02:00.065 --> 00:02:02.465
and says I'm gonna become
an expert at this thing.

00:02:02.465 --> 00:02:04.218
So how did you take that journey?

00:02:05.475 --> 00:02:08.795
- So we need to go back
to junior high school

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:13.795
when I was quite average
student in most respects.

00:02:14.115 --> 00:02:15.915
And I noticed that around that time,

00:02:15.915 --> 00:02:17.765
that's when we started getting grades

00:02:17.765 --> 00:02:19.065
on the tests that we were doing.

00:02:19.065 --> 00:02:22.325
And I found out that I
was sort of quite average

00:02:22.325 --> 00:02:23.695
and other students were doing better

00:02:23.695 --> 00:02:28.695
and I struggled to reach the
top of the grading scale.

00:02:31.972 --> 00:02:35.825
And so I assumed that in
order to get good results

00:02:35.825 --> 00:02:37.635
in school, you have to be very smart.

00:02:37.635 --> 00:02:38.615
And I had a worldview

00:02:38.615 --> 00:02:40.955
that some people were
born extremely intelligent

00:02:40.955 --> 00:02:42.085
and they did well

00:02:42.085 --> 00:02:44.875
and the rest didn't stand a
chance to do well in school.

00:02:44.875 --> 00:02:47.395
But then I also discovered
that those in class

00:02:47.395 --> 00:02:48.445
who got the good grades

00:02:48.445 --> 00:02:50.825
they have other study habits than I had,

00:02:50.825 --> 00:02:52.425
they had other study methods.

00:02:52.425 --> 00:02:56.275
And so I thought what
would happen if I adapted

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:57.108
the way I studied?

00:02:57.108 --> 00:03:00.225
So I started reading about how to learn

00:03:00.225 --> 00:03:04.095
and that eventually helped me
improve my results in school.

00:03:04.095 --> 00:03:06.735
I eventually graduated with
the highest grade point average

00:03:06.735 --> 00:03:08.365
in high school.

00:03:08.365 --> 00:03:10.798
I was admitted to the
University of Oxford.

00:03:12.172 --> 00:03:14.495
And, so I really benefited in my life

00:03:14.495 --> 00:03:16.155
from learning about learning.

00:03:16.155 --> 00:03:18.995
And once I was done with my education

00:03:18.995 --> 00:03:21.918
I wanted to help others
learn how to learn, so.

00:03:23.505 --> 00:03:28.425
- That's great, so what is something that

00:03:28.425 --> 00:03:31.285
back in those days that you actually tried

00:03:31.285 --> 00:03:34.155
and what you found to be helpful for you?

00:03:34.155 --> 00:03:38.325
- So back in those days,
typically when I was reading

00:03:38.325 --> 00:03:41.495
I thought that the
number of times you read

00:03:41.495 --> 00:03:43.305
the more you read something the better.

00:03:43.305 --> 00:03:45.825
So if I read through a chapter
three times, for example

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:46.741
that would be great.

00:03:46.741 --> 00:03:48.765
And that would be three times as good

00:03:48.765 --> 00:03:50.845
as if I only read through it once.

00:03:50.845 --> 00:03:52.955
What I discovered, however was that

00:03:52.955 --> 00:03:54.905
even after reading it three times

00:03:54.905 --> 00:03:56.615
I didn't necessarily remember it so much.

00:03:56.615 --> 00:03:59.005
And if I slowed down and stopped

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:01.025
to think about what I was reading,

00:04:01.025 --> 00:04:05.545
checking if I had understood
it, I retained much more.

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:07.495
And so that was just one small discovery

00:04:07.495 --> 00:04:09.385
and I made tons of these discoveries.

00:04:09.385 --> 00:04:12.605
I also tried a lot of things
that didn't work very well.

00:04:12.605 --> 00:04:16.475
For example, I listened to audio
books at night during sleep

00:04:16.475 --> 00:04:18.955
and thought that might be a
very efficient way to learn

00:04:18.955 --> 00:04:22.418
but I woke up very grumpy and tired.

00:04:24.065 --> 00:04:26.385
But I did a lot of this
experiment thing back and forth

00:04:26.385 --> 00:04:30.115
and eventually found some
really good ways to learn.

00:04:30.115 --> 00:04:34.005
- Yeah, I had a friend
who used to read her notes

00:04:34.005 --> 00:04:36.245
and then make similarly
play them when she slept.

00:04:36.245 --> 00:04:38.505
And she realized it was
actually not the playing

00:04:38.505 --> 00:04:39.455
them when she slept

00:04:39.455 --> 00:04:41.925
but it was like reading
through and saying the notes

00:04:41.925 --> 00:04:45.515
in her own words again, that
was actually helping her learn.

00:04:45.515 --> 00:04:47.425
And she didn't need to play
them while she was sleeping

00:04:47.425 --> 00:04:48.888
to get the benefit of that.

00:04:50.162 --> 00:04:51.895
(Olav mumbles)

00:04:51.895 --> 00:04:56.015
Exactly I also had that reading piece

00:04:56.015 --> 00:04:57.185
was my experience as well.

00:04:57.185 --> 00:04:59.068
Like you feel like if I just
highlight the right things

00:04:59.068 --> 00:05:01.765
and then keep read, rereading them

00:05:01.765 --> 00:05:03.735
that must get it into my brain

00:05:03.735 --> 00:05:06.908
and turns out that's not
how it works so much.

00:05:08.315 --> 00:05:10.805
- Well also what many people will do

00:05:10.805 --> 00:05:12.645
is that they paraphrase for example.

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:15.005
So they have something written in the book

00:05:15.005 --> 00:05:17.505
and then they just copy that
over to their own notes.

00:05:17.505 --> 00:05:20.015
And it can take a lot of time
and it takes a lot of effort

00:05:20.015 --> 00:05:23.115
but you might transfer something
over onto another paper

00:05:23.115 --> 00:05:25.968
but it doesn't transfer
into your long-term memory.

00:05:28.055 --> 00:05:32.615
- Yeah absolutely, so before
we get more into that,

00:05:32.615 --> 00:05:34.765
let me also ask so I know you did this

00:05:34.765 --> 00:05:38.665
language learning experiment
in Buenos Aires Argentina,

00:05:38.665 --> 00:05:40.995
tell us a little bit about that.

00:05:40.995 --> 00:05:43.565
- Yeah, so I've always been
interested in languages

00:05:43.565 --> 00:05:47.235
and foreign cultures, and I've
been wanting to learn Spanish

00:05:47.235 --> 00:05:51.065
for quite a long time actually,
but learning a language

00:05:51.065 --> 00:05:53.095
is such a massive big task.

00:05:53.095 --> 00:05:56.655
And I never really had
the time to focus on it

00:05:56.655 --> 00:05:58.885
and really pursue it.

00:05:58.885 --> 00:06:01.955
But then I had some time
right before the pandemic

00:06:01.955 --> 00:06:04.035
and I thought now I've spent so much time

00:06:04.035 --> 00:06:05.135
learning about learning.

00:06:05.135 --> 00:06:09.045
So I created a challenge for myself

00:06:10.265 --> 00:06:12.975
where I tried to see if I
could become fluent in Spanish

00:06:12.975 --> 00:06:16.325
in just one month by
applying the best techniques

00:06:16.325 --> 00:06:19.115
for learning that I had come across

00:06:19.115 --> 00:06:22.355
and by focusing on it fully
for one month and I did it

00:06:22.355 --> 00:06:24.995
and one week ago I released
a documentary about it.

00:06:24.995 --> 00:06:29.202
That's on YouTube in
case you wanna see how--

00:06:29.202 --> 00:06:32.222
- That's excellent, that is great

00:06:32.222 --> 00:06:35.415
that you got to do it right
before the pandemic hit

00:06:35.415 --> 00:06:37.895
reality to stay home,
so you're good timing.

00:06:37.895 --> 00:06:40.215
- Yeah that was actually excellent timing

00:06:40.215 --> 00:06:43.788
it was just like before
everything closed down.

00:06:44.735 --> 00:06:46.755
- Excellent that is great.

00:06:46.755 --> 00:06:49.715
Well, I as you said I caught it on YouTube

00:06:49.715 --> 00:06:54.385
as I was looking kinda doing
some research for this show

00:06:54.385 --> 00:06:57.525
and was interested to
see what you had done.

00:06:57.525 --> 00:06:59.838
So what prompted you to write this book?

00:07:01.465 --> 00:07:05.825
- So this book is a joint project

00:07:05.825 --> 00:07:09.385
between myself and Dr. Barbara Oakley

00:07:09.385 --> 00:07:11.825
who also written several
other books on learning.

00:07:11.825 --> 00:07:14.435
And we connected a few years ago

00:07:14.435 --> 00:07:18.345
we both published our first
book about learning in 2014

00:07:18.345 --> 00:07:20.548
and in both books did fairly well.

00:07:21.675 --> 00:07:26.388
And then a few years ago
we connected, we found,

00:07:27.465 --> 00:07:32.085
we sort of found a lot of common ground

00:07:32.085 --> 00:07:36.515
and we decided to write the
next book on how to learn

00:07:36.515 --> 00:07:39.515
taking into account
everything we have learned

00:07:39.515 --> 00:07:41.425
since the last book that
we've wrote about learning.

00:07:41.425 --> 00:07:45.355
And we also felt that
people now are so busy

00:07:45.355 --> 00:07:48.855
there's so much to do, there's
so many things to learn

00:07:48.855 --> 00:07:52.375
that there was a need for a
very concise to the point book

00:07:52.375 --> 00:07:54.405
that explains what you should do

00:07:55.736 --> 00:07:58.755
but also why, but in
a very concise manner.

00:07:58.755 --> 00:08:03.755
So we challenged ourselves
to write a very handy book

00:08:05.345 --> 00:08:08.145
and that book was published today.

00:08:08.145 --> 00:08:11.515
- Great, great so viewers
there was a reminder

00:08:11.515 --> 00:08:13.335
you can put questions in the chat here.

00:08:13.335 --> 00:08:17.285
So let's dive into some of
the pieces about learning.

00:08:17.285 --> 00:08:21.585
So one of the big pieces about learning

00:08:21.585 --> 00:08:25.235
is how do we move things from
kinda that working memory

00:08:25.235 --> 00:08:28.845
where it's active and we're
able to manipulate things

00:08:28.845 --> 00:08:32.445
into our long-term memory,
where we can access it later

00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:34.785
and think about where things are.

00:08:34.785 --> 00:08:37.815
What are some good ways
to be able to do that

00:08:37.815 --> 00:08:41.735
to get that information
into long-term memory?

00:08:41.735 --> 00:08:43.205
- So there are several of course

00:08:43.205 --> 00:08:47.115
but what psychology has found
is that one of the most robust

00:08:47.115 --> 00:08:51.715
and best techniques is
a retrieval practice.

00:08:51.715 --> 00:08:53.485
And it's funny because when we're learning

00:08:53.485 --> 00:08:57.485
we often want to place
things into long-term memory

00:08:57.485 --> 00:08:59.675
but it turns out the
best way to get things

00:08:59.675 --> 00:09:02.705
into long-term memory is to take it out

00:09:02.705 --> 00:09:06.675
retrieve it out as many times as possible.

00:09:06.675 --> 00:09:10.365
So retrieval practices,
can be really anything

00:09:10.365 --> 00:09:15.155
where you try to remember
what you've just read.

00:09:15.155 --> 00:09:17.915
It can be closing a book
after you read a chapter

00:09:17.915 --> 00:09:20.605
and then trying to recall
what you just read.

00:09:20.605 --> 00:09:25.605
It can be writing down
what you've just learned,

00:09:25.875 --> 00:09:28.425
it can be studying with flashcards.

00:09:28.425 --> 00:09:31.385
For example, you see you're
learning foreign vocabulary

00:09:31.385 --> 00:09:35.825
you see the word in English on one side

00:09:35.825 --> 00:09:37.165
and then you try to remember

00:09:37.165 --> 00:09:40.895
what the foreign language version was

00:09:40.895 --> 00:09:42.248
that's on the other side.

00:09:43.935 --> 00:09:45.335
And you can also discuss

00:09:45.335 --> 00:09:49.605
with others but then
without too much help,

00:09:51.935 --> 00:09:54.078
just try to recall from your own memory.

00:09:55.625 --> 00:09:58.585
- Yeah, it always strikes me

00:09:58.585 --> 00:10:00.265
that when you're trying
to remember something

00:10:00.265 --> 00:10:02.555
what you're doing is you're
pulling it out of memory.

00:10:02.555 --> 00:10:04.575
So that's what you should practice doing

00:10:04.575 --> 00:10:08.695
is pulling things out to
be able to access them

00:10:08.695 --> 00:10:11.105
that always (mumbles)

00:10:11.105 --> 00:10:14.695
- It's almost as if you
were storing something

00:10:14.695 --> 00:10:18.345
into the forest and it
can be easy to forget

00:10:18.345 --> 00:10:19.178
where it's stored

00:10:19.178 --> 00:10:22.035
but if you make a path
and you walk that path,

00:10:22.035 --> 00:10:24.695
back and forth enough times

00:10:24.695 --> 00:10:27.945
that vegetation will break down
and then you'll have a very

00:10:27.945 --> 00:10:31.945
solid path that makes it
easier for you to access,

00:10:31.945 --> 00:10:33.958
whatever you've stored there.

00:10:35.655 --> 00:10:38.845
And so it's really about
using what you learned

00:10:38.845 --> 00:10:41.675
not just passively absorbing or,

00:10:41.675 --> 00:10:43.445
but actually actively using it

00:10:43.445 --> 00:10:45.998
and bringing it out of long-term memory.

00:10:47.695 --> 00:10:50.655
- That makes sense so how do you,

00:10:50.655 --> 00:10:55.655
I know that Dr. Oakley does
a lot of neuroscience work

00:10:55.725 --> 00:10:58.805
and there's a lot of in
the book neuroscience basis

00:10:58.805 --> 00:11:00.335
for a lot of these things.

00:11:00.335 --> 00:11:05.005
What are some things that you
found that kind of explanation

00:11:05.005 --> 00:11:08.535
for that you found was really
helpful in understanding

00:11:08.535 --> 00:11:10.805
why maybe something works for learning?

00:11:10.805 --> 00:11:14.805
Is there an example of where
there's a good biological basis

00:11:14.805 --> 00:11:18.215
for the recommendation that you're making?

00:11:18.215 --> 00:11:22.505
- Yes I'd say, I mean over
and we put emphasis on that

00:11:22.505 --> 00:11:26.165
in the book also showing
why certain techniques work.

00:11:26.165 --> 00:11:28.215
And what I find is that
when you understand

00:11:28.215 --> 00:11:29.955
a little bit of the neuroscience

00:11:29.955 --> 00:11:33.005
it becomes much easier
to use good techniques

00:11:33.005 --> 00:11:34.985
because you get a much
richer understanding

00:11:34.985 --> 00:11:35.818
of the techniques.

00:11:35.818 --> 00:11:37.915
So you can apply them a little bit better

00:11:37.915 --> 00:11:41.295
in the right situations, extra.

00:11:41.295 --> 00:11:44.545
And so one of the things
that I found very helpful

00:11:46.755 --> 00:11:50.255
from neuroscience, which
Barb has been teaching

00:11:50.255 --> 00:11:52.525
for a few years is this difference

00:11:53.801 --> 00:11:57.685
between the task positive and
the task negative networks

00:11:57.685 --> 00:11:59.905
which we call the diffused

00:11:59.905 --> 00:12:02.325
and the focused mode of the brain.

00:12:02.325 --> 00:12:05.295
And basically what that means is that

00:12:05.295 --> 00:12:07.085
the brain has two different modes

00:12:07.085 --> 00:12:08.365
of thinking and learning.

00:12:08.365 --> 00:12:10.735
It has a mode which is very focused

00:12:10.735 --> 00:12:12.405
which is when you're working actively

00:12:12.405 --> 00:12:15.175
to solve a problem or to
think about something.

00:12:15.175 --> 00:12:17.645
But then it also has a mode which works

00:12:17.645 --> 00:12:20.585
when you are relaxing or
when you are not focusing

00:12:20.585 --> 00:12:24.185
intently on something which
we call the diffuse mode.

00:12:24.185 --> 00:12:28.185
And it's easy to think
that learning happens only

00:12:28.185 --> 00:12:29.985
when you're actively focused on something

00:12:29.985 --> 00:12:32.155
you're trying really
hard, but it turns out

00:12:32.155 --> 00:12:34.715
a lot of learning also
happens when you're relaxing

00:12:34.715 --> 00:12:37.725
because your brain
continues to work on it.

00:12:37.725 --> 00:12:42.035
So many students will think
that a good quality is to,

00:12:42.035 --> 00:12:44.305
don't give up and to persevere

00:12:44.305 --> 00:12:48.442
even if youre actually in
those types of situations

00:12:52.825 --> 00:12:55.585
what you need is this diffused mode

00:12:55.585 --> 00:12:57.625
that works when you are not focusing,

00:12:57.625 --> 00:13:00.515
because that can access information

00:13:00.515 --> 00:13:01.665
in a whole different way.

00:13:01.665 --> 00:13:06.665
It can help you connect ideas,
so sometimes taking a break

00:13:07.305 --> 00:13:09.678
can really help you while learning.

00:13:11.155 --> 00:13:13.845
And this is something
you've probably noticed

00:13:13.845 --> 00:13:14.945
as well that you were struggling

00:13:14.945 --> 00:13:17.555
with a problem in the
evening then you go to sleep

00:13:17.555 --> 00:13:19.475
and the next morning you have a solution.

00:13:19.475 --> 00:13:22.525
It's because this diffuse
mode keeps working

00:13:22.525 --> 00:13:23.475
on things in the background

00:13:23.475 --> 00:13:26.185
and you can use this
actually more day to day.

00:13:26.185 --> 00:13:28.495
If you're really struggling
with something you're learning

00:13:28.495 --> 00:13:31.748
take a break or do something
else and then continue later.

00:13:33.115 --> 00:13:35.025
Understanding these two modes for example,

00:13:35.025 --> 00:13:38.628
has helped me a lot in my own learning.

00:13:39.975 --> 00:13:42.868
- Awesome that is great, so is that like,

00:13:44.375 --> 00:13:46.675
is that part of the
explanation of why, when I

00:13:47.555 --> 00:13:51.205
used to walk my dog,
sometimes it would seem like

00:13:51.205 --> 00:13:52.715
I'm not paying attention to anything

00:13:52.715 --> 00:13:55.705
but then all of a sudden,
like when I'd come back

00:13:55.705 --> 00:13:57.555
I would seem to sit at my computer

00:13:57.555 --> 00:13:59.325
and have lots more
thoughts about the thing

00:13:59.325 --> 00:14:02.115
that I was working on instead of...

00:14:02.115 --> 00:14:04.505
- Exactly, because you might
think that you're relaxing

00:14:04.505 --> 00:14:07.398
you're taking a break and you aren't,

00:14:08.365 --> 00:14:11.655
in a way you are but your
brain continues to work

00:14:11.655 --> 00:14:14.005
with whatever you were
doing before that break.

00:14:15.025 --> 00:14:17.895
Another term for this and
psychology is incubation effect

00:14:17.895 --> 00:14:19.955
that, sometimes you have a problem

00:14:19.955 --> 00:14:23.055
something that needs to
incubate a little bit.

00:14:23.055 --> 00:14:24.255
And it's also what happens

00:14:24.255 --> 00:14:27.725
if you struggled to recall
the name of a person,

00:14:27.725 --> 00:14:29.375
you might see someone in
the stream and it's like,

00:14:29.375 --> 00:14:34.375
oh I know that person, but you
just can't recall the name.

00:14:34.425 --> 00:14:36.095
And then all of a sudden,
half an hour later

00:14:36.095 --> 00:14:38.685
it kind of falls down from the sky.

00:14:38.685 --> 00:14:41.565
It's that diffuse mode that's
been working in the background

00:14:41.565 --> 00:14:46.565
trying to find the name
within your long-term memory.

00:14:47.355 --> 00:14:51.005
- Got it, so that kind
of relates to a question

00:14:51.005 --> 00:14:55.125
that's just come in from
Kelvin Chin on Facebook

00:14:55.125 --> 00:14:58.175
it's asked how can we balance our hobbies

00:14:58.175 --> 00:14:59.478
with our studies?

00:15:00.445 --> 00:15:02.175
And thinking about those two

00:15:02.175 --> 00:15:03.815
maybe it's relating to those two modes

00:15:03.815 --> 00:15:07.811
but also thinking about, as we think about

00:15:07.811 --> 00:15:09.787
how much time should we be studying

00:15:09.787 --> 00:15:13.165
and how much time should we
maybe doing some other things.

00:15:13.165 --> 00:15:14.202
- Oh, that's a very good question

00:15:14.202 --> 00:15:16.325
and it depends on many things, of course

00:15:16.325 --> 00:15:18.745
but what we do know is
that if you are learning

00:15:18.745 --> 00:15:20.758
a lot of new information,

00:15:23.755 --> 00:15:25.505
a part of your brain
called the hippocampus

00:15:25.505 --> 00:15:28.655
can get overwhelmed if
you don't take breaks.

00:15:28.655 --> 00:15:30.355
So you might think that it's a good idea

00:15:30.355 --> 00:15:32.365
to sit for a very long stretch of time,

00:15:32.365 --> 00:15:36.195
but your brain struggles to
process all of this information

00:15:36.195 --> 00:15:39.145
and taking a few breaks helps the brain

00:15:40.435 --> 00:15:43.255
better stored this information
in long-term memory.

00:15:43.255 --> 00:15:46.785
So breaks are important for learning

00:15:46.785 --> 00:15:48.298
also from that perspective.

00:15:49.805 --> 00:15:54.215
And I think, how much time
you should spend learning

00:15:54.215 --> 00:15:58.105
it really depends on your ambitions

00:15:58.105 --> 00:16:03.105
and how much you have, how
difficult the material is.

00:16:04.185 --> 00:16:06.635
But I've always felt that if you do

00:16:06.635 --> 00:16:09.045
if you're a full-time
college student, for example

00:16:09.045 --> 00:16:11.395
and you sit then and work
for eight hours a day

00:16:11.395 --> 00:16:12.945
at the kind of normal work day,

00:16:14.105 --> 00:16:19.105
you should be satisfied with
your effort for that day.

00:16:19.175 --> 00:16:20.905
And there's actually research showing also

00:16:20.905 --> 00:16:24.785
that students who study
too much actually do worse.

00:16:24.785 --> 00:16:27.895
Because if you sit for too long of a time

00:16:27.895 --> 00:16:31.635
you also tend to become more ineffective.

00:16:31.635 --> 00:16:33.265
And at some point it tips

00:16:33.265 --> 00:16:35.415
if you just keep adding
more and more hours

00:16:36.645 --> 00:16:40.405
- That makes sense, but again
it comes back to those breaks

00:16:40.405 --> 00:16:44.225
and giving your brain a chance
to rest that'll make sense.

00:16:44.225 --> 00:16:47.255
So it also relates we're
heading into summer

00:16:47.255 --> 00:16:50.535
at least here in this hemisphere.

00:16:50.535 --> 00:16:54.355
And I know lots of parents
are thinking about,

00:16:54.355 --> 00:16:59.065
how much academic time should
they be spending this summer

00:16:59.065 --> 00:17:02.195
with their kids and thinking about,

00:17:02.195 --> 00:17:05.605
worried that they didn't
achieve everything they wanted

00:17:05.605 --> 00:17:06.655
through the last school year

00:17:06.655 --> 00:17:09.055
because of all of the pandemic closures.

00:17:09.055 --> 00:17:13.265
But as we think about learning
outside of the classroom

00:17:13.265 --> 00:17:16.395
are there particular tips and tricks

00:17:16.395 --> 00:17:20.125
when you maybe don't the whole structure

00:17:20.125 --> 00:17:22.555
of school around you,
but things you can do

00:17:22.555 --> 00:17:26.708
to still keep learning when
it's maybe a less formal space?

00:17:28.655 --> 00:17:31.225
- Yes, of course and there's
tons of things you can do.

00:17:31.225 --> 00:17:34.435
What I would say though, is
that, and we talked a little bit

00:17:34.435 --> 00:17:38.035
about this earlier, that a
lot of learning is hard work

00:17:39.125 --> 00:17:40.875
especially when you're enrolled in school

00:17:40.875 --> 00:17:42.005
and you have all this curriculum

00:17:42.005 --> 00:17:42.842
and you have to go through it

00:17:42.842 --> 00:17:44.905
and you only have this amount of time

00:17:44.905 --> 00:17:48.725
and the most efficient way to learn it

00:17:48.725 --> 00:17:50.865
might be a little bit hard.

00:17:50.865 --> 00:17:54.135
And so I would say that,
also during the summer

00:17:54.135 --> 00:17:58.765
it is important to take a
break and recharge a bit

00:17:58.765 --> 00:18:01.568
and to be ready for the new school year.

00:18:02.555 --> 00:18:05.745
And I think it's healthy
and good if the kids,

00:18:05.745 --> 00:18:07.555
at the end of the summer miss school

00:18:07.555 --> 00:18:11.175
and want to come back
rather than being exhausted

00:18:11.175 --> 00:18:16.175
but learning doesn't have to
be boring or hard work either.

00:18:16.925 --> 00:18:19.715
There's a lot of learning
that can happen that is fun,

00:18:19.715 --> 00:18:22.075
and that is an enjoyable activity.

00:18:22.075 --> 00:18:24.955
And so I would say,
especially during summer

00:18:24.955 --> 00:18:29.955
try to find things to learn,
to do that the students enjoy.

00:18:32.225 --> 00:18:34.625
And there's another added
benefit to this as well

00:18:34.625 --> 00:18:38.805
and that is that, especially
if you let a curiosity

00:18:38.805 --> 00:18:43.235
be an important driver,
so you choose yourself

00:18:43.235 --> 00:18:46.225
what you want to learn,
you follow your curiosity.

00:18:46.225 --> 00:18:49.515
If you do that, you actually
learn more effectively as well

00:18:49.515 --> 00:18:53.465
because getting your curiosity satisfied

00:18:55.135 --> 00:18:57.415
it leads to dopamine
production in the brain

00:18:57.415 --> 00:19:00.935
which strengthens memories
in long-term memory.

00:19:00.935 --> 00:19:05.935
So curiosity can be a very
strong driver for learning

00:19:06.675 --> 00:19:11.675
and letting curiosity lead
you is definitely recommended.

00:19:12.325 --> 00:19:15.745
- I love that, are there
ways that parents can help

00:19:15.745 --> 00:19:18.588
kinda nurture their kids' curiosity or,

00:19:20.415 --> 00:19:23.095
help urge them to ask those questions

00:19:23.095 --> 00:19:25.118
and to be curious about the world.

00:19:26.275 --> 00:19:29.475
- I think if you can
expose kids to novel things

00:19:29.475 --> 00:19:31.698
novel in environments,

00:19:33.109 --> 00:19:35.835
most kids already are
quite curious about things.

00:19:35.835 --> 00:19:37.335
So, I mean there's lots of things

00:19:37.335 --> 00:19:38.685
that you can do to stimulate that

00:19:38.685 --> 00:19:41.248
and also ask good questions.

00:19:43.315 --> 00:19:47.485
So that's, instead of just
going through content or,

00:19:47.485 --> 00:19:52.045
for example, if you are
discussing the First World War

00:19:52.045 --> 00:19:54.765
and the Second World War,
and you can talk about,

00:19:54.765 --> 00:19:56.705
you might dive straight into something

00:19:56.705 --> 00:19:58.705
and say, okay, now we're gonna cover how

00:19:59.574 --> 00:20:02.055
why the Second World War came about.

00:20:02.055 --> 00:20:03.895
But if you instead try to lead up

00:20:03.895 --> 00:20:06.035
with an interesting question, for example

00:20:06.035 --> 00:20:08.745
that the World War most
of Europe was in ruin

00:20:08.745 --> 00:20:12.025
and so many people had died
and countries were poor

00:20:12.025 --> 00:20:15.308
and so many families
have lost family members.

00:20:19.165 --> 00:20:22.055
Why did another major war breakouts?

00:20:22.055 --> 00:20:25.325
How could this happen when
you were just barely recovered

00:20:25.325 --> 00:20:28.125
from another major war?

00:20:28.125 --> 00:20:30.565
So you tried to find an
angle to where you can pose

00:20:30.565 --> 00:20:31.525
an interesting question.

00:20:31.525 --> 00:20:34.905
And then that curiosity is awakened

00:20:34.905 --> 00:20:36.085
it becomes more enjoyable

00:20:36.085 --> 00:20:37.685
and the learning is better as well,

00:20:37.685 --> 00:20:39.968
because once you've got curiosity,

00:20:41.735 --> 00:20:43.078
information sticks better.

00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:46.942
- Yeah, that's really, go ahead.

00:20:48.665 --> 00:20:52.328
- Adding choice, there
might be certain things

00:20:54.585 --> 00:20:55.515
that you cannot change.

00:20:55.515 --> 00:20:58.595
So we got to spend more time on on this

00:20:58.595 --> 00:21:01.155
and that's just how it got to be

00:21:01.155 --> 00:21:03.885
but there's a thousand ways
that you could do that.

00:21:03.885 --> 00:21:06.015
So you could, for example say,

00:21:06.015 --> 00:21:08.115
we should spend some time on
this, do you wanna do this?

00:21:08.115 --> 00:21:10.415
Do you wanna watch this documentary movie?

00:21:10.415 --> 00:21:12.425
Do you wanna go to that museum?

00:21:12.425 --> 00:21:16.995
And, do you want to us to
read this book together

00:21:16.995 --> 00:21:21.845
giving kids some choice
is also a great way

00:21:23.358 --> 00:21:25.488
to boost motivation for something?

00:21:26.945 --> 00:21:30.125
- So this ties, well I
think some of that might be

00:21:30.125 --> 00:21:31.305
some of the answer to this question

00:21:31.305 --> 00:21:34.385
but from Facebook Moniques ask,

00:21:34.385 --> 00:21:36.195
oftentimes it's a big jump for kids

00:21:36.195 --> 00:21:38.035
from elementary to middle school,

00:21:38.035 --> 00:21:39.335
and then middle school to high school

00:21:39.335 --> 00:21:41.795
where there's lots of rigorous home works

00:21:41.795 --> 00:21:43.212
and deadlines that kinda make them lose

00:21:43.212 --> 00:21:45.955
some of that interest in learning.

00:21:45.955 --> 00:21:49.125
Maybe even some of that curiosity
we've been talking about

00:21:49.125 --> 00:21:52.815
how can you work through that
and what would be your advice?

00:21:52.815 --> 00:21:55.015
- I mean, this is a real
challenge and research

00:21:55.015 --> 00:21:57.785
all around the world
shows that the more years

00:21:57.785 --> 00:22:01.392
kids are in school the
less motivated they become.

00:22:03.655 --> 00:22:08.285
So, the starting point, isn't a great one,

00:22:08.285 --> 00:22:10.705
but of course there are
many things that you can do

00:22:10.705 --> 00:22:14.335
and research on motivation
has come a long way too.

00:22:14.335 --> 00:22:17.685
For example, if you are
surrounded by other people

00:22:17.685 --> 00:22:19.355
who are motivated for something

00:22:19.355 --> 00:22:21.995
some of that motivation
can transfer to you

00:22:21.995 --> 00:22:26.975
that's called motivation
contagion in psychology.

00:22:26.975 --> 00:22:31.975
And so, expressing some
enthusiasm yourself

00:22:32.365 --> 00:22:34.135
with your kids, for what you're learning

00:22:34.135 --> 00:22:36.405
or teachers should keep this in mind, too.

00:22:36.405 --> 00:22:39.795
If they are really excited
about what they're teaching

00:22:39.795 --> 00:22:41.625
they can actually increase the motivation

00:22:41.625 --> 00:22:44.195
in their students as
well, so that's one thing.

00:22:44.195 --> 00:22:45.028
We're already talked about choice--

00:22:45.028 --> 00:22:48.555
- I'll just jump in and
say that Sal always says

00:22:48.555 --> 00:22:51.935
the best way to communicate
excitement about something

00:22:51.935 --> 00:22:54.338
is to actually be excited
yourself about it.

00:22:55.865 --> 00:22:57.795
Yeah, totally makes sense.

00:22:57.795 --> 00:23:00.785
- And I couldn't agree more
on neuroscience support sets.

00:23:00.785 --> 00:23:04.775
So, I mean that's great, we
already talked about choice.

00:23:04.775 --> 00:23:06.335
Perhaps you cannot decide,

00:23:06.335 --> 00:23:09.665
what we need to do but you can give

00:23:09.665 --> 00:23:10.935
students can not decide what to do

00:23:10.935 --> 00:23:13.685
but you can give them
choice in how to do it.

00:23:13.685 --> 00:23:16.435
And we also know that
mastery is very important

00:23:16.435 --> 00:23:19.975
for learning that the students feel that

00:23:19.975 --> 00:23:22.775
they actually learn and they progress.

00:23:22.775 --> 00:23:27.625
So paying a little bit
of attention to students'

00:23:27.625 --> 00:23:30.505
needs and making sure they get the help

00:23:30.505 --> 00:23:33.088
to master new things
is also very important,

00:23:33.985 --> 00:23:37.235
because if students have too
many bad experiences in a row

00:23:37.235 --> 00:23:39.485
they might lose faith in themselves

00:23:39.485 --> 00:23:40.755
and their abilities to learn

00:23:40.755 --> 00:23:45.755
and then they don't spend any effort.

00:23:46.936 --> 00:23:47.915
And then of course they don't learn

00:23:47.915 --> 00:23:50.435
and then it's just a vicious circle.

00:23:50.435 --> 00:23:55.435
- Right yeah, I talk a lot
about in motivation research

00:23:55.555 --> 00:23:59.445
there's this idea of, if you
think that you'll be successful

00:23:59.445 --> 00:24:01.805
when you do something
it's a lot more likely

00:24:01.805 --> 00:24:03.065
that you'll try to do that thing.

00:24:03.065 --> 00:24:05.125
And if you think you're
not gonna be successful

00:24:05.125 --> 00:24:06.105
if you think you're gonna fail

00:24:06.105 --> 00:24:08.825
you're not gonna be
motivated to do that thing.

00:24:08.825 --> 00:24:11.475
So it's expectancy of success which

00:24:11.475 --> 00:24:12.785
of course kind of makes sense.

00:24:12.785 --> 00:24:14.535
If you just think about it
who wants to do something

00:24:14.535 --> 00:24:15.755
you think you're gonna fail at

00:24:15.755 --> 00:24:18.085
but if we can help kids feel like

00:24:18.085 --> 00:24:20.925
they're gonna be successful
there's something there.

00:24:20.925 --> 00:24:23.585
- And there's a neural
underpinning for this as well

00:24:23.585 --> 00:24:27.005
because what gives you
this energy to expel effort

00:24:27.005 --> 00:24:29.175
to pursue a task is dopamine.

00:24:29.175 --> 00:24:31.145
And dopamine is released in your brain

00:24:31.145 --> 00:24:34.385
it gives you this just energy to act.

00:24:34.385 --> 00:24:36.495
But from an evolutionary perspective

00:24:36.495 --> 00:24:40.095
it's not very efficient
for us to be spending times

00:24:40.095 --> 00:24:43.595
on things that we will likely
not be able to succeed with.

00:24:43.595 --> 00:24:45.845
So to protect our survival

00:24:45.845 --> 00:24:50.045
if you are trying to catch
an eagle flying high up

00:24:50.045 --> 00:24:53.085
in the sky by throwing rocks,
you'll probably soon see

00:24:53.085 --> 00:24:55.275
that you cannot, that's not possible

00:24:55.275 --> 00:24:57.265
you cannot throw rocks that high.

00:24:57.265 --> 00:25:00.545
And so your brain will want
you to focus on something else

00:25:00.545 --> 00:25:02.525
give up on that task and do something else

00:25:02.525 --> 00:25:03.665
that is worthwhile.

00:25:03.665 --> 00:25:06.025
So it will cut the dopamine supply

00:25:06.025 --> 00:25:09.355
and you will lose
interest in pursuing that.

00:25:09.355 --> 00:25:12.835
And that's what happens
when in any type of activity

00:25:12.835 --> 00:25:17.185
if we feel that if we no longer
believe that we can succeed

00:25:17.185 --> 00:25:22.185
that dopamine level is
quickly lowered in the brain

00:25:22.605 --> 00:25:24.585
and then we lose the energy for it.

00:25:24.585 --> 00:25:27.655
Whereas if we through
either self-motivation

00:25:27.655 --> 00:25:29.065
or we have someone around us

00:25:29.065 --> 00:25:32.075
that gives us a little bit
of help or believes in us,

00:25:32.075 --> 00:25:34.875
if we get instilled that belief
again, that we can do it,

00:25:34.875 --> 00:25:37.825
dopamine production goes up
and we get that energy back

00:25:37.825 --> 00:25:39.245
and then we actually succeed with it

00:25:39.245 --> 00:25:43.588
because we also have the energy to pursue.

00:25:45.575 --> 00:25:49.635
- Fascinating, so we have
a question about your book

00:25:49.635 --> 00:25:51.555
from Facebook Manasseh Avani says

00:25:51.555 --> 00:25:54.945
is "Learn Like a Pro" good
for a fifth grader to read,

00:25:54.945 --> 00:25:58.385
if not, what would you
recommend for young people

00:25:58.385 --> 00:26:00.198
to learn about how to learn?

00:26:01.425 --> 00:26:05.925
- So for very young people I
would recommend my co-authors

00:26:05.925 --> 00:26:09.595
other book "Learning How to Learn"

00:26:09.595 --> 00:26:13.065
that book is especially
written kids and teens.

00:26:13.065 --> 00:26:16.475
It has a very simple and friendly language

00:26:16.475 --> 00:26:18.938
and lots of fun metaphors.

00:26:20.135 --> 00:26:22.665
It's probably a book many
kids can read themselves,

00:26:22.665 --> 00:26:24.385
but definitely a book they can read

00:26:24.385 --> 00:26:26.065
together with their parents.

00:26:26.065 --> 00:26:30.115
So that would be my best
bet for young learners.

00:26:30.115 --> 00:26:33.545
- Great, so it seems to me
that a lot of these things

00:26:33.545 --> 00:26:37.025
that we're talking about are
skills to practice the same way

00:26:37.025 --> 00:26:39.395
we practice other kinds of skills,

00:26:39.395 --> 00:26:42.845
like learning how to ride your bike

00:26:42.845 --> 00:26:44.085
or do those kinds of things.

00:26:44.085 --> 00:26:45.455
Is learning to learn,

00:26:45.455 --> 00:26:49.645
should we be having our kids
practice learning behaviors

00:26:49.645 --> 00:26:50.758
in similar ways?

00:26:51.715 --> 00:26:52.895
- Yes, we should and I mean,

00:26:52.895 --> 00:26:55.975
there's good evidence that
some students are better

00:26:55.975 --> 00:26:59.195
at picking up good study techniques

00:26:59.195 --> 00:27:02.035
and good study habits on their own.

00:27:02.035 --> 00:27:03.315
They're experimenting a little bit,

00:27:03.315 --> 00:27:04.995
they're reflecting about how they learn.

00:27:04.995 --> 00:27:07.815
And whereas other students might be stuck

00:27:07.815 --> 00:27:10.305
with very ineffective ways to study

00:27:10.305 --> 00:27:12.175
but that doesn't mean
that they can't improve

00:27:12.175 --> 00:27:14.985
because they can also
learn better techniques

00:27:14.985 --> 00:27:17.568
but they need a little bit of extra help.

00:27:19.115 --> 00:27:23.365
And so learning as a
skill is important as well

00:27:23.365 --> 00:27:26.305
and should be honed and
sort of throughout life,

00:27:26.305 --> 00:27:30.165
not only during early years,
but learning challenges

00:27:30.165 --> 00:27:32.925
and context keeps changing
throughout our lives.

00:27:32.925 --> 00:27:35.145
But one of the things that can help you

00:27:35.145 --> 00:27:38.735
in any learning situation
is being metacognitive

00:27:38.735 --> 00:27:41.065
which is a very fancy word for

00:27:41.065 --> 00:27:43.358
thinking about how you are thinking.

00:27:44.705 --> 00:27:46.685
And I mean research shows that some people

00:27:46.685 --> 00:27:49.085
does this more naturally,
they take a step back

00:27:49.085 --> 00:27:51.515
and they, look at how they're learning.

00:27:51.515 --> 00:27:53.815
And they're asking
themselves whether they are

00:27:55.045 --> 00:27:56.295
working in a smart way

00:27:56.295 --> 00:27:58.165
or whether they should change something.

00:27:58.165 --> 00:28:00.875
But this is also something
you can practice,

00:28:00.875 --> 00:28:02.795
with your kids, if
you're learning together

00:28:02.795 --> 00:28:06.205
and you can ask your kids questions

00:28:06.205 --> 00:28:09.835
about how they've been learning
that forces them to reflect

00:28:09.835 --> 00:28:14.015
for example, how are we gonna learn this?

00:28:14.015 --> 00:28:18.355
What do you suggest, why do
you want to learn in that way?

00:28:18.355 --> 00:28:20.925
And after you've had
a session, you can ask

00:28:20.925 --> 00:28:23.295
how did you think that this went?

00:28:23.295 --> 00:28:24.635
Is there something you would have done

00:28:24.635 --> 00:28:27.085
differently next time?

00:28:27.085 --> 00:28:28.225
So often in education

00:28:28.225 --> 00:28:32.745
we're very good at asking
questions about the content

00:28:32.745 --> 00:28:35.955
but we should spend a little bit more time

00:28:35.955 --> 00:28:38.835
about the process of learning
as well, how we are learning

00:28:38.835 --> 00:28:41.618
and reflecting and
thinking about that too.

00:28:43.655 --> 00:28:44.815
- That definitely makes sense

00:28:44.815 --> 00:28:49.175
and that process of how did it go?

00:28:49.175 --> 00:28:53.295
It strikes me there's some
potentially goal setting

00:28:53.295 --> 00:28:57.315
that is maybe helpful there
too, about at the beginning

00:28:57.315 --> 00:28:59.485
what do you want to learn?

00:28:59.485 --> 00:29:02.165
So that at the end, you
can say, did we get there?

00:29:02.165 --> 00:29:06.295
And where that is like
that kind of goal setting

00:29:06.295 --> 00:29:10.665
and reflection helpful based
on the research you've seen.

00:29:10.665 --> 00:29:12.905
- Yeah that's normally part of it.

00:29:12.905 --> 00:29:15.865
So in the beginning, you set a goal

00:29:15.865 --> 00:29:18.995
and you also try to understand
what the success criteria is

00:29:18.995 --> 00:29:23.125
or what sort of what you need to learn.

00:29:23.125 --> 00:29:27.075
And then you select some
tools or some strategies

00:29:27.075 --> 00:29:30.535
and which techniques you
should use depend completely

00:29:30.535 --> 00:29:32.385
on the learning challenge itself.

00:29:32.385 --> 00:29:34.405
One technique might be great for something

00:29:34.405 --> 00:29:36.105
but not work for something else.

00:29:36.105 --> 00:29:37.915
And that's also why
it's important to think

00:29:37.915 --> 00:29:39.952
of learning techniques
as a kind of toolkit

00:29:39.952 --> 00:29:43.005
and mental toolkit that
you keep developing

00:29:44.335 --> 00:29:46.235
so that you have strong tools

00:29:46.235 --> 00:29:49.878
but also the knowledge
about when to apply them.

00:29:50.905 --> 00:29:52.785
And then typically at the
end, you will look back

00:29:52.785 --> 00:29:56.095
and you will see whether
you reach your goal

00:29:56.095 --> 00:29:59.575
and whether you selected
the appropriate tool

00:29:59.575 --> 00:30:01.415
or whether you would want
to do something different

00:30:01.415 --> 00:30:02.248
than other time.

00:30:04.288 --> 00:30:07.075
- That makes sense, so
that seems like a good note

00:30:07.075 --> 00:30:09.195
to bee us on a mental toolkit

00:30:09.195 --> 00:30:12.935
that you developed throughout
your life, I liked it.

00:30:12.935 --> 00:30:15.198
Thank you so much for joining us today.

00:30:16.265 --> 00:30:19.095
Just brush the surface 30
minutes always goes by so fast

00:30:19.095 --> 00:30:22.895
but for people who were
interested, picked up the book

